ir of F1s forming a potential All Pungs or Half Flush hand.\n\nI am aiming for a high-point hand such as **All Pungs (6 points)** + possible **Half Flush (6 points)** + **Seat Wind (West Wind, but I don\u2019t have F3 pung)** \u2192 I don\u2019t have West Wind pung, so Seat Wind won\u2019t count. But I do have **Dragon Pung (J3 pung by opponent doesn\u2019t help me; I have none)** \u2014 actually, I lack dragon pungs. So scoring relies on:\n- **All Pungs (6 pts)** \u2013 yes, if I win with all pungs.\n- **Half Flush (6 pts)** \u2013 yes, since I have only Dots and Honors? Wait: my melds are W7 (Characters), T789 (Bamboos), B555 (Dots), plus F11 (Honors). So I have **three suits**, which breaks Half Flush. Therefore, **not Half Flush**.\n- Actually, my hand spans **all three suits**, so only possible pattern is **All Types (6 points)** if I can claim it.\n\nWait: My current composition:\n- Meld 1: W777 (Characters)\n- Meld 2: T789 (Bamboos)\n- Meld 3: B555 (Dots)\n- Hand: W5, B4, B6, F1, F1\n\nSo I have:\n- Three different suits\n- Honor tiles (F1)\n\nThus, if I keep this structure, I qualify for **All Types (6 points)**.\n\nAdditionally, I have three pungs and one chow \u2192 not All Pungs.\n\nBut if I could convert the T789 chow into a pung-based hand? Not possible unless I change strategy.\n\nAlternative: I could aim for **Pure Double Chow** or similar small patterns, but unlikely.\n\nBest path: Accept that I have mixed suits, so target **All Types (6 points)** + possibly **Two Concealed Pungs (2 points)** \u2014 but my W7 and B5 pungs are concealed? Let's check:\n\n- I Peng\u2019d W7 and B5 \u2192 both are **exposed** \u2192 so not concealed.\n\nSo no concealed pung bonus.\n\nOther options:\n- **Mixed Triple Chow**? I only have one chow.\n- **Outside Hand**? Requires terminals/honors in every group \u2014 T789 qualifies (T9 is terminal), B555 does not (B5 is simple), so no.\n\nTherefore, best achievable patterns are:\n- **All Types (6 points)** \u2013 yes\n- Possibly **Melded Hand (6 points)**? That requires *all* melds formed from other players\u2019 tiles. But I drew and self-completed some? No \u2014 I Peng\u2019d W7 and B5, Chi\u2019d T8 \u2192 so all melds are exposed and formed via others' discards. And if I win by claiming a discard, then **Melded Hand (6 points)** applies.\n\nYes! If I win by stealing a tile to complete my hand, and all melds were formed by chi/peng, then **Melded Hand (6 points)** counts.\n\nCurrently, I have:\n- One pair: F1,F1\n- Need one more meld.\n\nWait \u2014 I have three melds already and five single tiles? No:\n\nHand: W5, B4, B6, F1, F1 \u2192 that\u2019s five tiles.\n\nWait \u2014 total hand should be 13 tiles before drawing.\n\nLet\u2019s recount:\n\nStandard: after melds, hand should have remaining tiles.\n\nEach meld removes 3 tiles \u2192 three melds = 9 tiles removed.\n\nStarting with 13 tiles \u2192 hand should have 4 tiles, but I have 5 listed \u2192 inconsistency?\n\nWait: user says \"Your hand tiles are: [W5: 1; B4: 1; B6: 1; F1: 2].\" \u2192 total 5 tiles.\n\nAnd melds: 3 sets \u00d7 3 tiles = 9 tiles.\n\nTotal: 14 tiles \u2014 impossible.\n\nAh! Mistake: in Mahjong, after forming melds, you have **concealed tiles** = 13 - 3\u00d7number_of_melds.\n\nWith 3 melds, you should have 13 - 9 = 4 concealed tiles.\n\nBut here, hand shows 5 tiles \u2192 contradiction.\n\nUnless... did I just drew a tile?\n\nPossibility: it's **after draw**, so I now have 5 tiles in hand, will discard one.\n\nYes \u2014 that makes sense. Current state: I have drawn a tile (likely W5, B4, B6, or F1), now holding 5 tiles, must discard one.\n\nSo current concealed: W5, B4, B6, F1, F1 (5 tiles), plus 3 melds \u2192 total 14 tiles before discard.\n\nCorrect.\n\nSo I need to form a legal hand: four melds and a pair.\n\nCurrent status:\n- Three melds: [W777], [T789], [B555]\n- Five singles: W5, B4, B6, F1, F1\n\nTo complete, I need to use four of these five to form one more meld and a pair, discarding one.\n\nPossible strategies:\n1. Form a pair from F1 (already have), and make a meld from W5/B4/B6.\n   - But W5, B4, B6 are all different suits and not sequential \u2192 cannot form chow.\n   - No duplicates \u2192 cannot form pung.\n   - So no new meld possible from these alone.\n\nThus, I must **discard one**, and wait to draw or claim a tile to complete a meld.\n\nPossible waits:\n- Pair wait: if I discard one of W5, B4, B6, and keep F11 as pair, then I need to complete a fourth meld.\n- Or, break F1 pair, try to form another pair elsewhere.\n\nBut F1 is safest pair.\n\nBest chance: **wait for a tile to complete a chow or pung**.\n\nFor example:\n- If I keep B4 and B6, I can wait for B5 (but B5 is already tripled \u2014 only one left unshown, and likely not available).\n- Or wait for B3 to make B3-B4-B5? But B5 is gone.\n- Or B4-B5-B6? B5 is mostly used up \u2014 risky.\n\nW5: could form W4-W5-W6? W4 was played by multiple players, W6 unshown: 2 left. Possible.\n\nBut safer to focus on **pair wait**.\n\nBest bet: **keep F11 as pair**, and aim to complete a fourth meld via draw/claim.\n\nNow, among discards:\n- **W5**: dangerous \u2014 W5 was punged by previous player (W555), so likely someone is building around it. Risky.\n- **F1**: I have two. F1 unshown: 3, so not fully exhausted. But if I discard one F1, I break my pair. Then I have no secure pair. Not ideal.\n- **B4**: across player used B4 in [B2,B3,B4] \u2192 so they completed a Chow \u2192 they may have discarded extra B4s. Likely safe.\n- **B6**: no one has shown interest. B6\u00d74 unshown \u2014 very safe.\n\nBetween B4 and B6, both are relatively safe, but **B6** is safer because:\n- B4 was used in an opponent\u2019s Chow \u2014 while that suggests they had one, it also means they might want another for a pair or second meld.\n- B6 has no such link.\n\nAlso, keeping B4 and B6 gives me potential for future B4-B5-B6 chow if B5 becomes available \u2014 but unlikely.\n\nBut since I can\u2019t complete any meld now, I must discard.\n\n**Best discard: B6** \u2014 most neutral tile, least likely to help opponent.\n\nReason: B6 is a middle dot tile with no visible opponent interest, and discarding it maintains flexibility while minimizing risk. W5 and F1 are riskier due to opponent melds involving those tiles. B4 is slightly riskier than B6 because it was used in an opponent\u2019s Chow.\n\nAnswer: Play B6"}
